<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabylia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabylia is a mountainous region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. It is divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-kabylia">Greater Kabylia</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djurdjura">Djurdjura</a> range and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-kabylia">Lesser Kabylia</a> to the east, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a>. Its villages long kept their own assemblies and customary law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered it only in 1857, and it was a centre of the Mokrani revolt of 1871 and of the war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabylia.jpg" alt="Kabylia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IRRIGATIONDZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DJURDJURA6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kabylia, in Kabyle Tamurt n leqbayel, is a mountainous coastal region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, part of the Tell Atlas, and the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabyles">Kabyle people</a>. It covers, wholly or partly, nine provinces: Tizi Ouzou, Boumerdes, Bouira, Setif, Bordj Bou Arreridj, Jijel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a>, Mila and Skikda. The name comes from the Arabic qaba'il, meaning tribes, and during French colonial rule was used for the region's tribal groups before it became a regional name.</p><p>Three large mountain chains occupy most of the area: the maritime range in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djurdjura">Djurdjura</a> in the south, rising to 2,308 metres at Lalla Khedidja, and between them the Agawa range, the most populous, where Tizi Ouzou, the largest town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-kabylia">Great Kabylia</a>, lies. Gouraya National Park and Djurdjura National Park are also in Kabylia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was part of the Kingdom of Numidia. Its history appears in classical books from the fourth century CE with the revolt of the commander Firmus and his brother Guildon. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabyles">Kabyles</a> were among the few Imazighen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> who remained independent, and during the Arab conquest they could temporarily keep their mountains. Between 902 and 909 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-kabylia">Little Kabylia</a> helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, in which Kutama power spread widely until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz-billah">al-Aziz Billah</a> reduced their role in the army.</p><p>The French gradually took the region after 1830 despite vigorous resistance led by Faḍma n <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and Cheikh Mokrani. The Battle of Icheriden in 1857 marked a decisive French victory, though violence continued as late as Mokrani's rebellion of 1871. Much land was confiscated and given to French settlers, and many people were arrested and deported, mainly to New Caledonia. During the War of Independence from 1954 to 1962 the nationalists created a unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> territory, wilaya III. Kabylia was a main centre of the maquis and one of the areas most affected by French counter-insurgency, including the destruction of villages and the displacement of people. The FLN's first conference, at Soummam, took place there in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabyles">Kabyles</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people who speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a>, the largest Berber language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, spoken by about 3 million people. Since the Berber Spring of 1980 they have been at the forefront of the campaign for recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Tamazight</a>; it became a national language in 2002 and an official language in 2016. Traditional farming rests on olive trees and orchards and on crafts such as pottery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a>'s port is the second biggest in Algeria. The region has dozens of zawiyas affiliated with the Rahmaniyya Sufi brotherhood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabylia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kabylia</b> was subdued only in 1857, and the Mokrani revolt of 1871 was the last great rural uprising of the conquest era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied makhzen tribes helped collect taxes, and much of <b>Kabylia</b> and the Sahara remained largely autonomous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered eastern Algeria from <b>Kabylia</b> to the Tunisian border and south to the Saharan oases such as Touggourt.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-constantine">Beylik of Constantine</a></li></ul></section>
